# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **A command is one transaction, however many writes that holds.** As many rows to as many
# tables as one act needs — an invoice and its lines — and all of them commit together or
# none do. The transaction is opened here rather than left to each subclass, so the law is
# true by construction instead of by convention.
#
# It answers with a Result, and the class method enforces that — a subclass that answers
# with anything else stops the run rather than teaching its callers a second shape.
#
# **A command never calls another command.** Sequencing writes is a workflow's job, and a
# command that calls one has either nested this transaction or silently widened it, with
# nobody deciding which — becoming a workflow without accepting a workflow's bill.
class Command
  include TypedArguments

<%- if auth -%>
  extend Permission
<%- end -%>

  # Checked here once for every command, not in each `call` where one would forget it, and
  # before the transaction opens so a refusal costs no lock. An operation implementing
  # `anonymous_call` runs before anyone is identified and is not checked.
<%- if auth -%>
  def self.call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    unless permits?(actor)
      # The refusal is the entry nobody has when they need it.
      AuditLog.record(operation: name, outcome: :refused, actor: actor, error: :forbidden,
                      arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded_from_audit)
      return Result.failure(:forbidden)
    end

    result = perform(actor, arguments)

    # **After the transaction, never inside it.** An entry written inside is erased by the
    # rollback it exists to explain.
    AuditLog.record(
      operation: name,
      outcome: result.success? ? :succeeded : :failed,
      actor: actor,
      error: result.error,
      arguments: arguments,
      excluded: excluded_from_audit,
    )

    result
  end

  # **Setup asks what state is legal, not who may reach it**, so this skips the permission
  # check and the audit entry and nothing else — the two things about the caller rather than
  # the state. Typed arguments, the transaction and the rules still run, so what it produces is
  # a state the application can produce, which is the whole point of `no-test-factories`.
  #
  # **A test uses it for the subject too.** A permission is the class name, so authorisation is
  # class-sized and the check is this base class's, proven once in its own suite; an operation
  # has no authorisation behaviour of its own to assert.
  #
  # **It raises outside the test environment**, so it cannot become a production door. That is
  # the one thing an unchecked entry point must promise, and it is why this is a method rather
  # than an actor that says yes to everything: such an actor is a value, and a value can be
  # constructed anywhere, including in production.
  def self.test_call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    unless defined?(::Rails) && ::Rails.env.test?
      raise "#{name}.test_call is for tests: there is no unchecked door outside the test environment"
    end

    perform(actor, arguments)
  end

  # The work itself: everything that decides whether the state is legal, and nothing about who
  # asked for it. Both entry points share it, so a state reached in a test is reached the way
  # the application reaches it.
  def self.perform(actor, arguments)
    operation = new(**arguments)
    result = ActiveRecord::Base.transaction { operation.__perform__(actor) }
<%- else -%>
  def self.call(**arguments)
    result = ActiveRecord::Base.transaction { new(**arguments).__perform__ }
<%- end -%>

<%- if auth -%>
    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with a Result, got #{result.class}" unless result.is_a?(Result)

    result
  end
  private_class_method :perform
<%- else -%>
    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with a Result, got #{result.class}" unless result.is_a?(Result)

    # **After the transaction, never inside it.** An entry written inside is erased by the
    # rollback it exists to explain.
    AuditLog.record(operation: name, outcome: result.success? ? :succeeded : :failed,
                    error: result.error, arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded_from_audit)

    result
  end
<%- end -%>

  private
<%- if auth -%>

  # Whoever the door was opened for.
  attr_reader :actor

<%- end -%>

  # NOTE: `return` from inside a transaction block COMMITS it. Abandon work with
  # `raise ActiveRecord::Rollback`, and answer with `failure(:code)` afterwards.
  def success(value = nil)
    Result.success(value)
  end

  # **A failure carries a value, because a refusal often has to be rendered.** The caller
  # cannot re-read the request — it was parsed at the seam and is gone — so a form that did not
  # save comes back as a shape the edge places.
  def failure(error, value = nil)
    Result.failure(error, value)
  end

  # **Dispatches with an implicit receiver**, so it works whether the entry point is public or
  # private. `send` would too and is banned: a base class that writes it cannot ask an
  # application not to.
  #
  # Public and unreachable all the same — `new` is private, so nobody outside can hold an
  # operation to call this on.
<%- if auth -%>
  public def __perform__(actor)
    # **The actor belongs to the base class, not to the operation's signature.** Forcing
    # `actor:` into every initializer would put a keyword in fifty constructors so three could
    # read it. Set here, and available through the private reader below.
    @actor = actor

    self.class.anonymous? ? anonymous_call : call
  end
<%- else -%>
  public def __perform__
    call
  end
<%- end -%>

<%- if auth -%>
  # **One command, one transaction, one job** — the one grain at which a retry is safe, which
  # `a-command-runs-twice` is what makes harmless.
  #
  # **The Result describes the enqueue, never the work.** `success(:enqueued)` means accepted,
  # not done; there is no caller left to tell, which is why every door reports to the audit log.
  #
  # Permission is checked **twice, on purpose**: here so the caller learns it was refused, and
  # again when the job runs, because a right revoked in between is the answer that matters.
  def self.call_later(actor: nil, **arguments)
    unless permits?(actor)
      AuditLog.record(operation: name, outcome: :refused, actor: actor, error: :forbidden,
                      arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded_from_audit)
      return Result.failure(:forbidden)
    end

    # **Built and thrown away, so the arguments are asserted here.** Without it `call_later`
    # accepted what `call` refuses and answered `success(:enqueued)` for work that could never
    # run, burning its whole retry budget failing.
    new(**arguments)
    refuse_unidentifiable(actor)

    OperationJob.set(queue: queue_name).perform_later(
      operation: name,
      arguments: arguments,
      actor_class: actor&.class&.name,
      actor_id: actor.respond_to?(:id) ? actor.id : nil,
    )

    Result.success(:enqueued)
  end

  # **An actor that cannot be named cannot be deferred.** An actor with no `id` is legitimate
  # here — the door needs only `may?` — and used to be dropped silently: the caller was told
  # `success(:enqueued)`, and the job then died "requires an actor" and exhausted its retries.
  def self.refuse_unidentifiable(actor)
    return if actor.nil?
    return if actor.respond_to?(:id) && !actor.id.nil?

    raise ArgumentError, "#{name}.call_later needs an actor with an id to defer; " \
                         "#{actor.class} has none, so the job could not rebuild it"
  end

  private_class_method :refuse_unidentifiable

<%- else -%>
  # One command, one transaction, one job. The Result describes the enqueue, never the work.
  def self.call_later(**arguments)
    new(**arguments)

    OperationJob.set(queue: queue_name).perform_later(operation: name, arguments: arguments)

    Result.success(:enqueued)
  end
<%- end -%>
  # **Declared on the operation:** `QUEUE = :payments`, `ATTEMPTS = 2`. Read by OperationJob
  # when it decides whether to retry — not `retry_on`, which captures its limit when the job
  # class is defined and so fixes one policy for every operation.
  def self.queue_name
    const_defined?(:QUEUE, false) ? const_get(:QUEUE, false) : :default
  end

  private_class_method :queue_name

  # **The only way in is `call`**, so nothing can reach an operation that skipped the check,
  # the transaction or the return-type assertion. `allocate` is on the list because it is the
  # hole `new` alone leaves: public on every class, skips `initialize`, and
  # `SettleInvoice.allocate.__perform__(actor)` ran the operation unauthenticated.
  # Beyond what `PersonalData` declares: a value that is not a column and so is in no
  # registry — a token, a free-text note, a payload.
  def self.excluded_from_audit
    const_defined?(:EXCLUDE_FROM_AUDIT, false) ? const_get(:EXCLUDE_FROM_AUDIT, false) : []
  end

  private_class_method :excluded_from_audit

  private_class_method :new, :allocate
end
